window.docContents[80].resolve({"/2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory/2___3___-Representations/Irreducible-implies-indecomposable_SEMI_-main-problems-of-representation-theory/#Chapter2___Discussion_irreducible_vs_indecomposable___formalization":{"contents":"\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00092. Basic notions of representation theory\u00092.3. Representations\u0009Irreducible implies indecomposable; main problems of representation theory\u0009Irreducible implies indecomposable; main problems of representation theory","header":"Formalization","id":"/2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory/2___3___-Representations/Irreducible-implies-indecomposable_SEMI_-main-problems-of-representation-theory/#Chapter2___Discussion_irreducible_vs_indecomposable___formalization"},"/2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory/2___8___-Quivers/Bijection-between-representations-of-path-algebra-and-quiver/#Chapter2___Discussion_quiver_rep_bijection":{"contents":"We now justify our statement that a representation of a quiver is the same thing as a representation of the path algebra of a quiver.\n\nLet \\mathbf{V} be a representation of the path algebra P_Q. From this representation, we can construct a representation of Q as follows: let V_i = p_i \\mathbf{V}, and for any edge h, let x_h = a_h|_{p_{h'} \\mathbf{V}} : p_{h'} \\mathbf{V} \\longrightarrow p_{h''} \\mathbf{V} be the operator corresponding to the one-edge path h.\n\nSimilarly, let (V_i, x_h) be a representation of a quiver Q. From this representation, we can construct a representation of the path algebra P_Q: let \\mathbf{V} = \\bigoplus_i V_i, let p_i : \\mathbf{V} \\to V_i \\to \\mathbf{V} be the projection onto V_i, and for any path p = h_1 \\ldots h_m let a_p = x_{h_1} \\ldots x_{h_m} : V_{h'_m} \\to V_{h''_1} be the composition of the operators corresponding to the edges occurring in p (and the action of this operator on the other V_i is zero).\n\nIt is clear that the above assignments \\mathbf{V} \\mapsto (p_i \\mathbf{V}) and (V_i) \\mapsto \\bigoplus_i V_i are inverses of each other. Thus, we have a bijection between isomorphism classes of representations of the algebra P_Q and of the quiver Q.\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00092. Basic notions of representation theory\u00092.8. Quivers\u0009Bijection between representations of path algebra and quiver","header":"Bijection between representations of path algebra and quiver","id":"/2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory/2___8___-Quivers/Bijection-between-representations-of-path-algebra-and-quiver/#Chapter2___Discussion_quiver_rep_bijection"},"/3___-General-results-of-representation-theory/3___8___-The-Krull-Schmidt-theorem/Completion-of-proof-of-the-Krull-Schmidt-theorem/#Chapter3___Discussion_proof_of_Theorem3___8___1___formalization":{"contents":"\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00093. General results of representation theory\u00093.8. The Krull-Schmidt theorem\u0009Completion of proof of the Krull-Schmidt theorem\u0009Completion of proof of the Krull-Schmidt theorem","header":"Formalization","id":"/3___-General-results-of-representation-theory/3___8___-The-Krull-Schmidt-theorem/Completion-of-proof-of-the-Krull-Schmidt-theorem/#Chapter3___Discussion_proof_of_Theorem3___8___1___formalization"},"/4___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Basic-results/4___9___-Computing-tensor-product-multiplicities-using-character-tables/Tensor-product-multiplicities-for-S_3___-S_4___-and-A_5/#Chapter4___Example4___9___1___formalization":{"contents":"\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00094. Representations of finite groups: Basic results\u00094.9. Computing tensor product multiplicities using character tables\u0009Tensor product multiplicities for S_3, S_4, and A_5\u0009Tensor product multiplicities for S_3, S_4, and A_5","header":"Formalization","id":"/4___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Basic-results/4___9___-Computing-tensor-product-multiplicities-using-character-tables/Tensor-product-multiplicities-for-S_3___-S_4___-and-A_5/#Chapter4___Example4___9___1___formalization"},"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___18___-Schur-Weyl-duality-for-______mathfrak___gl____LPAR_V_RPAR____/Completion-of-proof-of-Theorem-5___18___2/#Chapter5___Discussion_Theorem5___18___2_proof_end":{"contents":"This completes the proof of the theorem. \\square\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00095. Representations of finite groups: Further results\u00095.18. Schur-Weyl duality for $\\mathfrak{gl}(V)$\u0009Completion of proof of Theorem 5.18.2","header":"Completion of proof of Theorem 5.18.2","id":"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___18___-Schur-Weyl-duality-for-______mathfrak___gl____LPAR_V_RPAR____/Completion-of-proof-of-Theorem-5___18___2/#Chapter5___Discussion_Theorem5___18___2_proof_end"},"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___22___-The-characters-of-___L____lambda___/L____lambda___1___N___-___-L_lambda-___-______N-V/#Chapter5___Proposition5___22___2":{"contents":"Proposition 5.22.2. The representation L_{\\lambda + 1^N} (where 1^N = (1, 1, \\ldots, 1) \\in \\mathbb{Z}^N) is isomorphic to L_\\lambda \\otimes \\wedge^N V.\n\nProof. Indeed, L_\\lambda \\otimes \\wedge^N V \\subset V^{\\otimes n} \\otimes \\wedge^N V \\subset V^{\\otimes n+N}, and the only component of V^{\\otimes n+N} that has the same character as L_\\lambda \\otimes \\wedge^N V is L_{\\lambda + 1^N}. This implies the statement. \\square\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00095. Representations of finite groups: Further results\u00095.22. The characters of $L_\\lambda$\u0009L_{lambda+1^N} ≅ L_lambda ⊗ Λ^N V","header":"L_{lambda+1^N} ≅ L_lambda ⊗ Λ^N V","id":"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___22___-The-characters-of-___L____lambda___/L____lambda___1___N___-___-L_lambda-___-______N-V/#Chapter5___Proposition5___22___2"},"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___3___-Frobenius-divisibility/Section-5___3___-Frobenius-divisibility/#Chapter5___Introduction_5___3___heading-1":{"contents":"","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00095. Representations of finite groups: Further results\u00095.3. Frobenius divisibility\u0009Section 5.3: Frobenius divisibility\u0009Section 5.3: Frobenius divisibility","header":"5.3. Frobenius divisibility","id":"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___3___-Frobenius-divisibility/Section-5___3___-Frobenius-divisibility/#Chapter5___Introduction_5___3___heading-1"},"/6___-Quiver-representations/6___1___-Problems/Problem-6___1___6___-McKay-graph/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--6___-Quiver-representations--6___1___-Problems--Problem-6___1___6___-McKay-graph--Problem-6___1___6___-McKay-graph--Formalization--Supporting-declarations":{"contents":"An auxiliary classification property holds for the integer pairing under the stated finiteness, size, and nontriviality hypotheses.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter6/Problem6.1.6; role=supporting]* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter6/Problem6.1.6/Derived4; role=supporting]* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter6/Problem6.1.6/Derived5; role=supporting]* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter6/Problem6.1.6/Derived7; role=supporting]\n\nEach row of the auxiliary integer matrix has dimension-weighted sum zero.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter6/Problem6.1.6; role=supporting]* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter6/Problem6.1.6/Derived4; role=supporting]* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter6/Problem6.1.6/Derived5; role=supporting]* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter6/Problem6.1.6/Derived7; role=supporting]\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00096. Quiver representations\u00096.1. Problems\u0009Problem 6.1.6: McKay graph\u0009Problem 6.1.6: McKay graph\u0009Formalization","header":"Supporting declarations","id":"/6___-Quiver-representations/6___1___-Problems/Problem-6___1___6___-McKay-graph/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--6___-Quiver-representations--6___1___-Problems--Problem-6___1___6___-McKay-graph--Problem-6___1___6___-McKay-graph--Formalization--Supporting-declarations"},"/6___-Quiver-representations/6___4___-Roots/Lemma-6___4___2___-B-is-positive-definite-and-even-valued/#Chapter6___Lemma6___4___2___formalization":{"contents":"\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00096. Quiver representations\u00096.4. Roots\u0009Lemma 6.4.2: B is positive definite and even-valued\u0009Lemma 6.4.2: B is positive definite and even-valued","header":"Formalization","id":"/6___-Quiver-representations/6___4___-Roots/Lemma-6___4___2___-B-is-positive-definite-and-even-valued/#Chapter6___Lemma6___4___2___formalization"},"/7___-Introduction-to-categories/7___3___-Morphisms-of-functors/Examples-of-natural-transformations/#Chapter7___Example7___3___2___formalization":{"contents":"\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00097. Introduction to categories\u00097.3. Morphisms of functors\u0009Examples of natural transformations\u0009Examples of natural transformations","header":"Formalization","id":"/7___-Introduction-to-categories/7___3___-Morphisms-of-functors/Examples-of-natural-transformations/#Chapter7___Example7___3___2___formalization"},"/7___-Introduction-to-categories/7___7___-Abelian-categories/Finitely-generated-modules-over-finitely-generated-commutative-ring/#Chapter7___Problem7___7___3___formalization":{"contents":"\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00097. Introduction to categories\u00097.7. Abelian categories\u0009Finitely generated modules over finitely generated commutative ring\u0009Finitely generated modules over finitely generated commutative ring","header":"Formalization","id":"/7___-Introduction-to-categories/7___7___-Abelian-categories/Finitely-generated-modules-over-finitely-generated-commutative-ring/#Chapter7___Problem7___7___3___formalization"},"/7___-Introduction-to-categories/7___9___-Exact-functors/Exactness-properties-of-Ind___-Res___-Hom___-tensor-product/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--7___-Introduction-to-categories--7___9___-Exact-functors--Exactness-properties-of-Ind___-Res___-Hom___-tensor-product--Exactness-properties-of-Ind___-Res___-Hom___-tensor-product--Formalization--Supporting-declarations":{"contents":"Exactness is preserved by the induced map on balanced tensor quotients when the second map is\nonto.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter7/Example7.9.6; role=supporting]\n\nThe balanced-quotient map induced by multiplication by two over the integers with left factor\nZMod 2 is not injective.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter7/Example7.9.6; role=supporting]\n\nInduction from a finite-index subgroup preserves finite limits and finite colimits.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter7/Example7.9.6; role=supporting]\n\nFor every integer-linear map from the integers to ZMod 2, postcomposition with that map is\nnot surjective on linear maps with source ZMod 2.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter7/Example7.9.6; role=supporting]\n\nRestriction of representations along a group homomorphism preserves finite limits and finite\ncolimits.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter7/Example7.9.6; role=supporting]\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00097. Introduction to categories\u00097.9. Exact functors\u0009Exactness properties of Ind, Res, Hom, tensor product\u0009Exactness properties of Ind, Res, Hom, tensor product\u0009Formalization","header":"Supporting declarations","id":"/7___-Introduction-to-categories/7___9___-Exact-functors/Exactness-properties-of-Ind___-Res___-Hom___-tensor-product/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--7___-Introduction-to-categories--7___9___-Exact-functors--Exactness-properties-of-Ind___-Res___-Hom___-tensor-product--Exactness-properties-of-Ind___-Res___-Hom___-tensor-product--Formalization--Supporting-declarations"},"/9___-Structure-of-finite-dimensional-algebras/9___4___-Homological-dimension/Projective-dimension/#Chapter9___Definition9___4___1":{"contents":"Definition 9.4.1. The projective dimension \\operatorname{pd}(M) of M is the length of the shortest finite projective resolution of M (where we agree that the length of 0 \\to P_d \\to \\cdots \\to P_0 is d). If a finite projective resolution of M does not exist, then the projective dimension of M is infinite.\n\nFor instance, the projective modules are the modules of projective dimension 0.\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00099. Structure of finite dimensional algebras\u00099.4. Homological dimension\u0009Projective dimension","header":"Projective dimension","id":"/9___-Structure-of-finite-dimensional-algebras/9___4___-Homological-dimension/Projective-dimension/#Chapter9___Definition9___4___1"}});